1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,264
The seventh of March, ten minutes to midnight.

2
00:00:03,314 --> 00:00:08,544
Tomorrow is Women's Day, and Malika's shop has never been busier.

3
00:00:08,594 --> 00:00:11,976
Five buyers press Pay at the same moment.

4
00:00:12,026 --> 00:00:17,376
Each payment fits her daily limit alone, but together they go far over it.

5
00:00:17,426 --> 00:00:24,192
There is a worse night too: someone steals the shop's account, and creates one payment for a fortune.

6
00:00:24,242 --> 00:00:36,240
In Lesson 21, each website's deal set the most it may take per payment and per day, and in Lesson 27, one claim made sure a card was charged only once.

7
00:00:36,290 --> 00:00:42,504
This lesson makes those limits hold, even when five buyers pay at the same moment.

8
00:00:42,554 --> 00:00:48,768
Payment limits are the most a website may take in one payment, and the most in one day.

9
00:00:48,818 --> 00:00:53,040
The bank sets both for each website, as part of its deal.

10
00:00:53,090 --> 00:00:55,056
The first limit is simple.

11
00:00:55,106 --> 00:00:59,592
A payment over the most per payment is refused the moment it is created.

12
00:00:59,642 --> 00:01:04,608
That holds whether the merchant's server creates it, or a payment link does.

13
00:01:04,658 --> 00:01:08,976
The daily limit is harder, because buyers race each other.

14
00:01:09,026 --> 00:01:14,592
Most teams check it the obvious way: read the day's total, and then charge.

15
00:01:14,642 --> 00:01:18,240
I think that check is worse than none, because it looks safe.

16
00:01:18,290 --> 00:01:24,432
All five charges read the total before any of them is added, so all five go through.

17
00:01:24,482 --> 00:01:31,640
So how can a check stop five buyers who all read the total at the same moment?

18
00:01:31,690 --> 00:01:35,768
So here, each charge makes an atomic reservation.

19
00:01:35,818 --> 00:01:39,344
Atomic means one step that cannot be split.

20
00:01:39,394 --> 00:01:47,840
In that one step, the charge adds its amount to the day's total, but only if the new total still fits under the limit.

21
00:01:47,890 --> 00:01:52,952
When two charges arrive together, one waits until the other is done.

22
00:01:53,002 --> 00:01:57,392
The one that no longer fits fails before any money moves.

23
00:01:57,442 --> 00:02:04,232
The reservation is saved together with the charge's own record, before the card network is ever asked.

24
00:02:04,282 --> 00:02:08,192
If the card is declined, its share goes back to the day.

25
00:02:08,242 --> 00:02:11,648
A reversal on the same day gives it back too.

26
00:02:11,698 --> 00:02:17,336
A payment whose outcome is unknown keeps its share until someone knows what happened.

27
00:02:17,386 --> 00:02:21,488
And the day is the same business day the daily reports add up.

28
00:02:21,538 --> 00:02:29,432
In front of all of this stands the edge: the first server every request meets when it arrives from the internet.

29
00:02:29,482 --> 00:02:33,272
It accepts only modern encrypted connections.

30
00:02:33,322 --> 00:02:36,776
It tells browsers to use secure connections only.

31
00:02:36,826 --> 00:02:39,392
It never names the software it runs.

32
00:02:39,442 --> 00:02:45,680
It refuses requests that are too large, and it limits how fast one address may call.

33
00:02:45,730 --> 00:02:53,456
A week before the holiday, Dilnoza opens Malika's website in the bank's portal and goes to its deal terms.

34
00:02:53,506 --> 00:02:58,232
Two fields sit side by side: Most per payment, and Most per day.

35
00:02:58,282 --> 00:03:01,664
A hint says that empty means no limit.

36
00:03:01,714 --> 00:03:05,624
She enters the bank's chosen amounts and saves.

37
00:03:05,674 --> 00:03:12,968
In Malika's portal, the website now shows its limits: so much a payment, so much a day.

38
00:03:13,018 --> 00:03:18,200
Bekzod tries a payment link for an amount above the per payment limit.

39
00:03:18,250 --> 00:03:21,368
The portal shows a red notice: Not done.

40
00:03:21,418 --> 00:03:25,784
It says this website takes at most that amount in one payment.

41
00:03:25,834 --> 00:03:27,992
No link is created.

42
00:03:28,042 --> 00:03:30,896
Now the last minutes before midnight.

43
00:03:30,946 --> 00:03:33,464
Five buyers press Pay together.

44
00:03:33,514 --> 00:03:37,856
Four charges fit, and go through one after another.

45
00:03:37,906 --> 00:03:39,944
The fifth no longer fits.

46
00:03:39,994 --> 00:03:47,384
Its payment fails with a plain message: the website has reached its limit for today, and no money was taken.

47
00:03:47,434 --> 00:03:51,992
The stolen account cannot move a fortune in one payment.

48
00:03:52,042 --> 00:03:55,616
The busy night cannot quietly go over the deal.

49
00:03:55,666 --> 00:04:01,616
And when a limit stops a buyer, it stops them before their card is charged, never after.

50
00:04:01,666 --> 00:04:08,192
To recap: each limit holds, because the daily one is reserved in one atomic step.

51
00:04:08,242 --> 00:04:12,808
So what happens to the fifth buyer's money?

52
00:04:12,858 --> 00:04:16,984
Nothing: the limit stops them before any money moves.

53
00:04:17,034 --> 00:04:21,520
Next: how the system is installed, starting with its keys.
